Let's find this limit directly.

To do that, we will want to divide both the numerator and the denominator by the same quantity, in a way that will help us derive the limit.

Since the leading term of the numerator is $x^4$, let's divide by $x^4$.

In the denominator, let's divide by $\sqrt{x^8}$, since for any  value, $x^4=\sqrt{x^8}$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{2x^4-7}{\sqrt{4x^8+7x^5}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{2x^4-7}{x^4}}{\dfrac{\sqrt{4x^8+7x^5}}{\sqrt{x^8}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide sides by }x^4=\sqrt{x^8}}
\end{align}$

Now let's continue by simplifying the expression and using the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{2\cancel{x^4}}{\cancel{x^4}}-\dfrac{7}{x^4}}{\sqrt{\dfrac{4\cancel{x^8}}{\cancel{x^8}}+\dfrac{7\cancel{x^5}}{\cancel{x^5}\cdot x^3}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{2-\dfrac{7}{x^4}}{\sqrt{4+\dfrac{7}{x^3}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{2-0}{\sqrt{4+0}}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt{4}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2}{2}
\\\\
&=1
\end{align}$
